# modules/network/checks-transport.rules - the NET-10 script-check registry
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; data/scoursh-network-scan-design/report.md
# §3.3, §5.1, §5.2, the NET-10 row in its §7 staged plan). Owned by
# modules/network/transport.sh; ids are both `NET-TRANSPORT-*`.
#
# ONE FILE PER OWNING PHASE SCRIPT, per rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's
# `checks-<name>.rules` row (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29) - matching every
# sibling registry in this directory, never a shared `checks.rules` two
# peers could collide on.
#
# TWO CHECK IDS, ONE PER PROPERTY, for the identical reason every sibling
# registry in this directory gives: the `net` fingerprint location profile
# (lib/findings.sh, target host port transport) carries no component naming
# WHICH transport defect a finding is about, so a plaintext-protocol
# exposure and a STARTTLS-not-required observation on the same listener
# would collide onto one fingerprint if they shared an id - and they are
# genuinely different facts with different remediations, not one fact
# reported at two strengths (report.md §3.3's own framing: the first is "the
# service answers in the clear at all", the second is "TLS is offered but
# not enforced").
#
# A THIRD id for "expired/self-signed certificate on a non-web listener" -
# the third case report.md §3.3's prose names - is DELIBERATELY ABSENT
# HERE: modules/network/tlsport.sh (NET-08) already assesses every
# non-base-url listener's certificate unconditionally under its own
# NET-TLS-CERT_EXPIRED-01/NET-TLS-SELF_SIGNED-01 ids, and "non-base-url"
# already means "non-web" for this module. See
# modules/network/transport_engine.sh's own header for the full reasoning;
# adding a third id here would collide onto the identical fact NET-08
# already reports under a different check id at the same location.
#
# `coverage-scope: target` is NET's cell (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5.1's own
# NET row), identical to every other NET-* check.
#
# THE TYPE TAG IS `passive`, MATCHING modules/network/engine.sh's OWN
# `transport.sh:passive` PHASE-TABLE FLOOR - every reused primitive this
# check calls (a TCP connect classification, one TLS handshake, one
# zero-byte banner read) is one a sibling phase already tags `passive` for
# the identical reason: connect-and-observe, never a protocol command this
# scanner composes and sends of its own. `requires-cmd: openssl` on both
# ids matches the phase's own dependency check - the plaintext-service
# check needs it directly for the native-TLS-absence handshake, and the
# STARTTLS check depends on that same classification before it ever runs,
# so it is withheld under the identical absence.

id: NET-TRANSPORT-PLAINTEXT_SERVICE-01
title: Network service answers in the clear on a port whose protocol has a standard encrypted variant
script: transport.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Run this service's encrypted variant instead (its standard
  implicit-TLS port), or require STARTTLS on this same port before any
  credential or protocol data is exchanged, and disable the plaintext
  listener once every real client has migrated. A client that authenticates
  against this service today is sending its credentials and data
  unencrypted to anyone positioned on the network path. Port identification
  here is by number alone, which is why this finding is reported at medium
  confidence rather than high - verify the listening service before acting.
references: CWE-319
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: NET-TRANSPORT-STARTTLS_NOT_REQUIRED-01
title: Network listener advertises STARTTLS but does not appear to require it
script: transport.sh
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Configure this service to require STARTTLS before any protocol
  command runs, rather than offering it opportunistically (Postfix
  `smtpd_tls_security_level = encrypt`, Dovecot `disable_plaintext_auth = yes`
  with `ssl = required`, ProFTPD `TLSRequired on`), or retire this port in
  favour of its implicit-TLS variant entirely. This scan did not attempt
  authentication and so could not confirm whether AUTH itself is blocked
  without STARTTLS; verify enforcement directly against the service's own
  configuration.
references: CWE-319
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/
